           "fact": "F1",
           "source": "alg-anchored-irgnm-with-discrepancy-stopping",
           "source_section": "Definition",
-          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $F:U\\to Y$ be continuously Fréchet differentiable between real or complex Hilbert spaces, with open $U\\subseteq X$. Inputs are anchor and initial point $x_0\\in U$, datum $y_\\delta$, noise bound $\\delta\\ge0$, discrepancy factor $\\tau>1$, finite update cap $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$, $\\alpha_0>0$ and $q\\in(0,1)$. For $k=0,\\ldots,N$, first check $x_k\\in U$; otherwise return domain-failure. Evaluate $F(x_k)$; if $\\|F(x_k)-y_\\delta\\|\\le\\tau\\delta$, return $(x_k,k,\\texttt{discrepancy})$. If $k=N$, return $(x_k,k,\\texttt{budget-exhausted})$. Otherwise set $A_k=F'(x_k)$, $\\alpha_k=\\alpha_0q^k$, solve\n$$(A_k^*A_k+\\alpha_k I)s_k=A_k^*(y_\\delta-F(x_k))+\\alpha_k(x_0-x_k),$$\nand set $x_{k+1}=x_k+s_k$. The unique Hilbert-space solve exists by [[lem-hilbert-tikhonov-normal-operator-invertible]]. An evaluation failure returns evaluation-failure with the last valid state; a finite implementation that cannot certify the exact normal solve returns solve-unverified. No success or convergence claim is attached to these failure statuses.\n\nThe mathematical extended recurrence means the same exact updates with the discrepancy and finite-cap tests suppressed, for as long as the states lie in $U$. It is used only for comparison in proofs. A finite actual run follows its prefix and stops at its first test crossing. This anchored update is distinct from the retained unanchored Levenberg-Marquardt step; its extra term is $\\alpha_k(x_0-x_k)$.",
+          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $X,Y$ be Hilbert spaces over the same field\n$\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, let $U\\subseteq X$ be open, and let\n$F:U\\to Y$ be continuously Fréchet differentiable over $\\mathbb F$. In the\ncomplex case, each $F'(x)$ is a bounded complex-linear operator and the\nFréchet remainder is measured over complex increments, with\n$$\\lim_{\\substack{h\\to0,\\ h\\ne0\\\\x+h\\in U}}\n\\frac{\\|F(x+h)-F(x)-F'(x)h\\|}{\\|h\\|}=0.$$\nInputs are anchor and\ninitial point $x_0\\in U$, datum $y_\\delta$, noise bound $\\delta\\ge0$,\ndiscrepancy factor $\\tau>1$, finite update cap $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$,\n$\\alpha_0>0$ and $q\\in(0,1)$. For $k=0,\\ldots,N$, first check $x_k\\in U$;\notherwise return domain-failure. Evaluate $F(x_k)$; if\n$\\|F(x_k)-y_\\delta\\|\\le\\tau\\delta$, return\n$(x_k,k,\\texttt{discrepancy})$. If $k=N$, return\n$(x_k,k,\\texttt{budget-exhausted})$. Otherwise set\n$A_k=F'(x_k)$, $\\alpha_k=\\alpha_0q^k$, solve\n$$(A_k^*A_k+\\alpha_k I)s_k=A_k^*(y_\\delta-F(x_k))+\\alpha_k(x_0-x_k),$$\nand set $x_{k+1}=x_k+s_k$. The unique Hilbert-space solve exists by [[lem-hilbert-tikhonov-normal-operator-invertible]]. An evaluation failure returns evaluation-failure with the last valid state; a finite implementation that cannot certify the exact normal solve returns solve-unverified. No success or convergence claim is attached to these failure statuses.\n\nThe mathematical extended recurrence means the same exact updates with the discrepancy and finite-cap tests suppressed, for as long as the states lie in $U$. It is used only for comparison in proofs. A finite actual run follows its prefix and stops at its first test crossing. This anchored update is distinct from the retained unanchored Levenberg-Marquardt step; its extra term is $\\alpha_k(x_0-x_k)$.",
           "uses": [
             "1.2"
           ]
@@ -2070,7 +2078,7 @@
         {
           "id": "step-1-1",
           "step": "1.1",
-          "claim": "For $h=x-u$, the segment lies in $B$, so [F3] gives $F(x)-F(u)-Ah=\\int_0^1(F'(u+sh)-A)h\\,ds$. Its integrand has norm at most $\\kappa\\|Ah\\|$, hence the integral has that bound. The triangle inequality and reverse triangle inequality give the two-sided estimate. Subtracting $(F'(u)-A)h$ bounds the linearization remainder by $2\\kappa\\|Ah\\|$, proving the cone constant. This integrates the continuous vector $F'(u+sh)h$, not a possibly unmeasurable choice of the operators $R_{u+sh}$. Taking $u=x^\\dagger$ also gives $\\|t\\|\\le2\\kappa v$.",
+          "claim": "For $h=x-u$, let $\\gamma(s)=F(u+sh)$. The segment lies in $B$. If $h\\ne0$ and $0<s<1$, then for real $r\\to0$ with $s+r\\in[0,1]$, same-field Fréchet differentiability gives\n$$\\left\\|\\frac{F(u+(s+r)h)-F(u+sh)}r-F'(u+sh)h\\right\\|\n=\\frac{\\|F(u+(s+r)h)-F(u+sh)-F'(u+sh)(rh)\\|}{|r|}\\longrightarrow0.$$\nThus $\\gamma'(s)=F'(u+sh)h$. For $h=0$ the curve is constant. In either case differentiability makes $\\gamma$ continuous by the Fréchet remainder definition, and this derivative is continuous on the segment with endpoint extensions because the assumed continuous Fréchet differentiability makes $s\\mapsto F'(u+sh)$ continuous in operator norm, including at $s=0,1$, and evaluation at the fixed vector $h$ is continuous. Applying the Banach-valued fundamental theorem [F3] and subtracting the constant integral of $Ah$ gives\n$$F(x)-F(u)-Ah=\\int_0^1(F'(u+sh)-A)h\\,ds.$$\nLinearity of $R_{u+sh}$ and its norm bound make the integrand norm at most $\\kappa\\|Ah\\|$, so the integral has that bound. The triangle and reverse triangle inequalities give the two-sided estimate. Subtracting $(F'(u)-A)h$ bounds the linearization remainder by $2\\kappa\\|Ah\\|$, proving the cone constant. This integrates the continuous vector $F'(u+sh)h$, not a possibly unmeasurable choice of the operators $R_{u+sh}$. Taking $u=x^\\dagger$ also gives $\\|t\\|\\le2\\kappa v$.",
